INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261018-scotus-term-cases', 'supreme-court-2026-27-term-cases-to-watch', 'newsroom', 'analysis', 'courts', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Supreme Court 2026-27 Term: The Biggest Cases to Watch and When They''re Argued', 'AR-15 bans, voter citizenship rules, climate lawsuits and religious liberty headline the justices'' new term.', 'The Supreme Court''s 2026-27 term, known formally as October Term 2026, opened on Monday, Oct. 5, 2026. The biggest cases scheduled so far involve state bans on AR-15-style rifles, proof-of-citizenship rules for voter registration, climate-change lawsuits against energy companies, religious preschools and immigration enforcement. Most decisions are expected by the end of the term in late June or early July 2027.

## When did the Supreme Court''s 2026-27 term begin?

The term began on **Oct. 5, 2026**, the first Monday in October, as the court''s own argument calendar shows. Arguments are grouped into sittings, with several argument days a week.

The court posts its monthly argument calendars on supremecourt.gov. So far it has released calendars for October, November and the session that spans late November and December. Additional cases granted this fall are likely to be argued in early 2027.

## Is this a big Supreme Court term?

Early assessments describe it as somewhat calmer than the last one. Zach Shemtob, executive editor of SCOTUSblog, told the Deseret News that the term is quieter than recent ones but noted the justices could still add high-profile cases. The previous term included major rulings on tariffs and birthright citizenship, according to the Deseret News.

Even so, the current docket already includes cases on gun rights, voting, religion and immigration that could affect laws in many states, including Massachusetts.

## What are the biggest Supreme Court cases of 2026-27?

The table below lists major cases with argument dates confirmed on the court''s official calendars.

| Argument date | Case | Issue |
|---|---|---|
| Oct. 5, 2026 | Suncor Energy v. Commissioners of Boulder County | Whether federal law blocks local climate-damage lawsuits against energy companies |
| Oct. 13, 2026 | Beaird v. United States | Sentencing enhancement tied to large-capacity magazines |
| Nov. 3, 2026 | St. Mary Catholic Parish v. Roy | Religious preschools and Colorado''s universal preschool program |
| Nov. 4, 2026 | Guerrero v. Johnson | Limits on repeat challenges in a death-penalty case |
| Nov. 30, 2026 | Crowther v. Board of Regents of the University System of Georgia | Whether employees can sue for sex discrimination under a federal education law |
| Dec. 1, 2026 | Jouppi v. Alaska | Whether seizing an airplane over a small alcohol violation is an excessive fine |
| Dec. 2, 2026 | Viramontes v. Cook County; Grant v. Higgins | Whether bans on AR-15s and similar rifles violate the Second Amendment |
| Dec. 7, 2026 | International Partners for Ethical Care v. Ferguson | Parents'' standing to challenge Washington state shelter-care laws |
| Dec. 8, 2026 | Republican National Committee v. Mi Familia Vota | Arizona''s proof-of-citizenship requirement for voter registration |
| Dec. 9, 2026 | Department of Homeland Security v. D.V.D. | Deportations to countries other than a migrant''s home country |
| Dec. 9, 2026 | Grand v. City of University Heights | Permits for prayer gatherings in a private home |

## What is the AR-15 case at the Supreme Court?

The justices will hear two consolidated cases on **Dec. 2**, with one hour set aside for argument, according to the court''s calendar.

SCOTUSblog reported that the cases challenge a semiautomatic-rifle ban in Cook County, Illinois, and a similar Connecticut law passed after the 2012 Sandy Hook school shooting. Lower courts upheld both bans, and the justices agreed to review them on June 30, 2026.

The core question is whether the Second Amendment protects the right to own these rifles. According to the Deseret News, limits on large-capacity magazines are not directly at issue in these cases. Massachusetts has its own restrictions on assault-style weapons, which are also part of a referendum on this year''s state ballot, so the ruling is being watched closely in the state.

## What voting case is the Supreme Court hearing?

In **Republican National Committee v. Mi Familia Vota**, set for **Dec. 8**, the court will consider whether Arizona may require applicants who use the state''s voter-registration form to prove U.S. citizenship.

SCOTUSblog reported the case also asks whether the National Voter Registration Act allows states to remove suspected noncitizens from voter rolls within 90 days of a federal election. The Deseret News noted that similar ideas appear in the SAVE Act, a Republican bill in Congress.

## What religion cases are on the 2026-27 docket?

Religion is a major theme this term. Key cases include:

- **St. Mary Catholic Parish v. Roy (Nov. 3):** SCOTUSblog reported that Colorado offers families 15 free hours of preschool a week at participating schools that agree to serve children regardless of characteristics including sexual orientation and gender identity. Catholic preschools argue the requirement violates their religious freedom; the state defends its nondiscrimination rule.
- **Grand v. City of University Heights (Dec. 9):** An Orthodox Jewish man in Ohio was told he needed a city permit to host a prayer group in his home. The question is whether officials violated protections against religious discrimination, SCOTUSblog reported.

Several religion-related petitions are also pending. SCOTUSblog identified challenges to Louisiana and Texas laws requiring Ten Commandments displays in public-school classrooms, a request to overrule a 2004 precedent allowing states to exclude devotional degrees from scholarship programs, and a California homeschooling dispute. The court has not yet agreed to hear those.

## What climate and immigration cases are on the docket?

**Suncor Energy v. Boulder County**, argued on opening day, asks whether communities can make energy companies pay for local costs they attribute to climate change. Boulder County, Colorado, has sued Suncor and Exxon Mobil, according to the Deseret News; the companies argue the Clean Air Act gives authority over greenhouse gases to the federal government.

On immigration, **Department of Homeland Security v. D.V.D.**, set for Dec. 9, concerns the government''s policy of deporting noncitizens to third countries. **Wassily v. Blanche**, argued Nov. 30, asks whether people whose asylum status is later ended can still become lawful permanent residents, SCOTUSblog reported.

The Deseret News also flagged **Barbosa da Cunha v. Rhoney**, which involves detaining noncitizens arrested inside the U.S. without the option of release on bond. That case does not yet appear on the court''s published argument calendars.

## Which criminal-justice cases should Massachusetts readers watch?

- **Beaird v. United States (Oct. 13):** A dispute over a federal sentencing enhancement for possessing a large-capacity magazine, which the Deseret News said also puts a 1993 precedent on judicial deference to sentencing guidelines under review.
- **Guerrero v. Johnson (Nov. 4):** A capital case over when a prisoner may bring a later challenge based on a legal rule.
- **Kian v. Florida:** The Deseret News reported this case asks whether the Sixth Amendment requires 12-person juries in criminal trials. It said Massachusetts is among a handful of states that use six-person juries in some criminal cases. The newspaper said argument is likely in 2027.

## When will the Supreme Court decide these cases?

The court typically releases opinions in argued cases by the end of June or early July. The Deseret News reported that a ruling in the Boulder climate case is expected by June 2027. Decisions can come any time after argument, but the most contested cases often arrive near the end of the term.

## Frequently asked questions

### When does the Supreme Court hear the AR-15 case?

The consolidated cases Viramontes v. Cook County and Grant v. Higgins are scheduled for argument on Dec. 2, 2026. The court''s calendar allots one hour for the combined argument.

### Where can I find the Supreme Court''s argument schedule?

The court posts monthly argument calendars on supremecourt.gov. Calendars released so far cover arguments from Oct. 5 through Dec. 9, 2026.

### What is the most important Supreme Court case this term?

Legal analysts differ. SCOTUSblog and the Deseret News have highlighted the AR-15 cases, the Arizona voter-citizenship case and immigration detention disputes as among the most significant.

### Does the Supreme Court hear arguments on holidays?

No. The court''s calendars show no arguments on Oct. 12 or Nov. 11, both federal holidays.

### Will the Supreme Court rule on Ten Commandments laws this term?

Not yet decided. SCOTUSblog reported that petitions challenging Louisiana and Texas classroom-display laws are pending, and the justices have not announced whether they will hear them.', 'Boston Daily Staff', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'newsroom', 'content/2026-10-18-guides-long.sql', 6, 0, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-10-18T19:30:00.000Z', '2026-10-18T19:30:00.000Z');
