INSERT OR IGNORE INTO story_events (id, story_id, from_status, to_status, actor, note, created_at) VALUES ('bd-20261018-scotus-term-cases-ev1', 'bd-20261018-scotus-term-cases', NULL, 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 'newsroom-assistant', 'In-depth explainer drafted from official sources; awaiting editor approval', '2026-10-18T19:30:00.000Z');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261018-ma-ballot-questions', 'massachusetts-ballot-questions-2026-explained-yes-no-vote', 'newsroom', 'analysis', 'states', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Massachusetts Ballot Questions 2026 Explained: What a Yes or No Vote Means', 'A plain-language guide to all nine statewide questions on the Nov. 3 ballot, from public records to marijuana and the gun law.', 'Massachusetts voters will decide nine binding statewide ballot questions on Nov. 3, 2026. Questions 1 through 8 are proposed laws that reached the ballot by citizen petition, covering public records, primaries, voter registration, state revenue, conservation funding, housing and recreational marijuana. Question 9 asks whether to keep the 2024 state gun law: a yes vote keeps it and a no vote repeals it.

## How many ballot questions are on the 2026 Massachusetts ballot?

There are **nine statewide questions**, according to the Secretary of the Commonwealth''s Elections Division. Some voters will also see non-binding public policy questions that appear only in certain legislative districts.

The state''s official Information for Voters guide includes a summary, full text, fiscal statement and arguments for and against each statewide question. It is available online at sec.state.ma.us.

## How do Massachusetts ballot questions work?

The nine statewide questions fall into two categories:

- **Initiative petitions (Questions 1–8):** These are new laws proposed by initiative petition. A yes vote approves the proposed law; a no vote rejects it.
- **Referendum (Question 9):** Voters are asked whether they approve of a law the Legislature already passed. If a majority votes no, the Elections Division says the law will be repealed.

Each question''s official summary includes a fiscal statement from the Executive Office of Administration and Finance, which estimates the effect on state and local budgets.

## What does a yes or no vote mean on each question?

| Question | Topic | A YES vote would | A NO vote would |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Public records | Bring most records of the Legislature and governor''s office under the Public Records Law | Make no change |
| 2 | CPCS collective bargaining | Let Committee for Public Counsel Services employees unionize and bargain | Make no change |
| 3 | All-party primary | Replace party primaries for state offices with a single primary; top two advance | Make no change |
| 4 | Election Day registration | Let eligible residents register or update their address at the polls on Election Day | Make no change |
| 5 | State revenue limit | Tie the state''s revenue cap to prior-year revenue and wage growth, with rebates above it | Make no change |
| 6 | Nature for All Fund | Create a conservation fund supported by state taxes on sporting goods, recreational vehicles and golf courses | Make no change |
| 7 | Single-family homes | Allow single-family homes on residential lots of at least 5,000 square feet | Make no change |
| 8 | Recreational marijuana | Repeal legal retail sales of adult-use marijuana | Make no change |
| 9 | 2024 firearms law | Keep the 2024 gun law in place | Repeal the 2024 gun law |

## What is Question 1 on public records?

Question 1 would make most records held by the Legislature and the governor''s office subject to the state Public Records Law. The proposal exempts records used in developing public policy and certain communications between lawmakers and constituents seeking help with government services. It would take effect Jan. 1, 2027. State finance officials found no significant budget effect.

NBC Boston reported the measure is backed by State Auditor Diana DiZoglio.

## What is Question 2 on public defender unions?

Question 2 would let employees of the Committee for Public Counsel Services, the state agency that provides lawyers to people who cannot afford one, form a union and bargain collectively. After reaching a contract, the agency would have to ask the governor to fund it. Administration and Finance said the cost is difficult to project because it depends on future bargaining.

## What is Question 3 on primaries?

Question 3 would replace separate party primaries for state offices with one primary in which all candidates share a ballot and the top two finishers advance to November. Candidates for governor and lieutenant governor would run jointly in that primary. Any group whose statewide candidates win at least 3% of primary ballots would gain party status. It would take effect Jan. 1, 2028.

NBC Boston reported supporters say it reduces insider control, while opponents argue it could shut out third-party and independent candidates.

## What is Question 4 on Election Day registration?

Question 4 would allow eligible residents to register, or update their address, at their polling place on Election Day by showing proof of residency and signing an oath. People without proof could cast a provisional ballot that counts only if they later provide documents by a deadline. Voters could not change party affiliation at the polls. It would take effect Jan. 1, 2028.

According to NBC Boston, the state Republican Party opposes the measure, citing security concerns.

## What is Question 5 on the state revenue limit?

Question 5 would cap annual state revenue at the prior year''s net revenue grown by the three-year average growth in Massachusetts wages and salaries. Collections above the cap would be refunded to taxpayers the next year, and revenue from the surtax on incomes over $1 million would count toward the cap. It would take effect July 1, 2027.

Administration and Finance said stricter limits would reduce money available for the state budget, including local aid for schools and municipalities, and for the state''s rainy-day Stabilization Fund.

## What is Question 6, the Nature for All Fund?

Question 6 would create a fund for natural-resource conservation, subject to legislative appropriation, drawing on state taxes collected on sporting goods, recreational vehicles and golf courses. A 15-member board would set rules for spending. It would take effect July 1, 2027. NBC Boston reported the measure is backed by Mass Audubon.

## What is Question 7 on single-family homes?

Question 7 would allow single-family homes in residential zones on lots of at least 5,000 square feet with at least 50 feet of street frontage and access to public water and sewer. Cities and towns could still regulate height, setbacks, open space, parking and short-term rentals. NBC Boston reported the measure excludes Boston and that the Massachusetts Municipal Association opposes it as a statewide mandate.

## What is Question 8 on recreational marijuana?

Question 8 would repeal the laws that legalize, regulate and tax retail sales of adult-use marijuana, effective Jan. 1, 2028. Adults 21 and older could still possess up to 1 ounce. Licensed adult-use businesses could apply to become medical marijuana dispensaries, and the Cannabis Control Commission would regulate only the medical market.

Administration and Finance said adult-use sales taxes raised about $280 million in state revenue and $50 million in local revenue in fiscal 2025, revenue that would be lost if the measure passes.

## What is Question 9 on the gun law?

Question 9 is a referendum on the firearms law the Legislature approved on July 18, 2024. Among other things, the law:

- lets school administrators and licensed health care providers seek extreme risk protection orders
- sets penalties for untraceable "ghost guns" and requires most firearms to have serial numbers
- requires purchasers of semiautomatic rifles and shotguns to be at least 21
- expands the definition of machine guns to include devices such as bump stocks
- restricts carrying firearms in government buildings, polling places and schools, with exceptions

A **yes** vote keeps the law; a **no** vote repeals it. Administration and Finance said repeal would require new state spending to unwind investments already made to carry out the law. NBC Boston reported the law took effect despite the repeal petition because the governor declared an emergency.

## Frequently asked questions

### Does a yes vote on Question 9 keep or repeal the gun law?

A yes vote keeps the 2024 firearms law in place. A no vote repeals it, according to the Secretary of the Commonwealth.

### When would the ballot questions take effect if passed?

It depends on the question. Question 1 would take effect Jan. 1, 2027; Questions 5 and 6 on July 1, 2027; and Questions 3, 4 and 8 on Jan. 1, 2028, according to the official summaries.

### Would Question 8 make marijuana illegal to possess in Massachusetts?

No. Adults 21 and older could still possess up to 1 ounce, according to the official summary. The measure would end legal retail sales of adult-use marijuana and change penalties for larger amounts.

### Where can I read the full text of each ballot question?

The full text, summaries and arguments for and against appear in the state''s Information for Voters guide at sec.state.ma.us. Printed copies in Spanish, Chinese, Vietnamese and Khmer can be requested by calling 1-800-462-VOTE.

### Why are there extra questions on some ballots?

Some districts have non-binding public policy questions that instruct local legislators. Topics include abolishing Norfolk County government, a carbon fee and single-payer health care; they have no legal effect.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/John_F._Kennedy_statue_outside_the_Capitol,_Boston,_Massachusetts_LCCN2011630418.jpg/1280px-John_F._Kennedy_statue_outside_the_Capitol,_Boston,_Massachusetts_LCCN2011630418.jpg', 'Photo: Carol M. Highsmith / Wikimedia Commons, public domain', 'public_domain', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-10-18-guides-long.sql', 6, 0, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-10-18T19:30:00.000Z', '2026-10-18T19:30:00.000Z');
